# Line endings are normalised in the repository and checked out as-is.
#
# Without this, editing on Windows commits CRLF into files the rest of the
# tree stores as LF, and the difference shows up as a whole-file diff the next
# time anyone touches them on another platform. The shell scripts matter most:
# a .sh with CRLF fails at the shebang with "bad interpreter", which is an
# obscure way to discover a line-ending problem.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Genuinely binary, so never touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.mp4 binary
*.jar binary
*.pem -text

# Windows scripts are run by Windows tools, which want CRLF.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.xml text eol=crlf
